# Each function runs once and stores behind the definition, in one branch. The call stands behind a test of the closure, and ways on which nothing was stored lead to that test.
# The test goes with the way that stored, and the code does not say so: the verifier asks the closure that is made in the branch, not the read, the call, or the closure that a copy of the reader makes.
# The scope of counted() lists a value where it is made and is asked at its closure. The scope of early() lists none: its one variable has a dead zone, and the closure stands in front of every store.
